    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>A gravel driveway is a classic, good-value and attractive choice for many homes, particularly in rural and suburban settings. Unlike asphalt or concrete, which can crack and need professional repair, gravel is forgiving and easy to look after if you keep to a regular routine. The key to a long life, though, is staying ahead of problems. Without a seasonal plan, a gravel drive can quickly develop potholes, ripples (often called "washboarding") and drainage trouble.</p>
<p>Each season brings its own challenges. Understanding them lets you keep the driveway looking smart and working well for many years. This guide sets out what to do through the year, and when to call in your installer.</p>

<h2 id="spring-inspection">1. Spring inspection</h2>
<p>Spring is arguably the most important time for gravel driveway care. As the ground thaws and the wet weather sets in, the structure of the drive is at its most vulnerable. Your aim in spring is to assess what the winter's freeze-thaw cycles and snow clearing have done.</p>
<p>Start by walking the whole length of the driveway and looking for areas where the stone has moved. Look for frost heave, where freezing ground has swelled and pushed stones out of line, and for thin spots where the sub-base or the soil beneath is starting to show through. Deal with these promptly, before they turn into deep potholes.</p>
<p>Spring is also the time to check drainage. A gravel drive should have a slight crown: a gentle fall from the centre to the edges so rainwater runs off to the sides. Your installer sets the exact fall for your drive. If the surface has flattened out, water will pool in the middle and break down the layers underneath quickly.</p>

<h2 id="pothole-repair">2. Pothole repair</h2>
<p>Potholes are the main enemy of a gravel driveway. Left alone, they collect rainwater, which softens the sub-base, and the hole grows every time a car drives over it. Simply raking loose gravel into a hole is a temporary fix that rarely lasts more than a few days.</p>
<p>To repair a pothole properly:</p>
<ul>
<li>Dig it out a little to remove loose stones, debris and mud, so you are working down to firm material.</li>
<li>Fill it with crushed stone that includes fines (the stone dust that binds it together), not smooth, rounded stones. In the UK this is sold as Type 1 sub-base or MOT Type 1; in the US it is known as crusher run or "crush and run". Its angular edges lock together when compacted.</li>
<li>Fill in thin layers of roughly 5 cm (2 in), compacting each one firmly with a hand tamper before adding the next.</li>
<li>Finish the patch slightly proud of the surrounding surface to allow for settling, then top with stone that matches the rest of the drive.</li>
</ul>
<p>If the same pothole keeps coming back, the problem is probably drainage or a weak sub-base underneath, and it is worth asking your installer to look at it.</p>

<h2 id="summer-weeding">3. Summer weeding</h2>
<p>As the weather warms, organic matter that has settled between the stones gives weeds and grass somewhere to grow. A few sprouts may look harmless, but roots can loosen the compacted layers and make the drive more prone to erosion.</p>
<p>Keep on top of weeds through the summer:</p>
<ul>
<li>Pull young weeds by hand or with a long-handled weeding tool while they are small.</li>
<li>If you use a weedkiller, choose one labelled for paths and driveways, follow the label exactly and keep it well away from drains, ponds and watercourses.</li>
<li>A flame weeder works on young growth, but follow the manufacturer's instructions and never use it in dry conditions or near plants, hedges, fences, vehicles or fuel.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you find yourself weeding constantly, the gravel layer may be too thin, letting seeds reach the soil below. A top-up (see below) often helps.</p>

<h2 id="dust-management">4. Dust management</h2>
<p>In a hot, dry spell, a gravel drive can throw up a lot of dust. This is more than a cosmetic nuisance: the dust is made of the fines that bind the surface together. When they blow away, the larger stones loosen and the surface becomes less stable.</p>
<p>A light watering with a hose during long dry spells keeps dust down and helps the surface stay compact. On long or busy rural drives, some contractors apply dust suppressants such as calcium chloride or magnesium chloride. If you consider one, ask your contractor first, follow the product label and check any local rules, particularly near wells, streams or planting.</p>

<h2 id="autumn-clearing">5. Autumn clearing</h2>
<p>Autumn care is mostly about debris. Fallen leaves look lovely, but as they break down they turn into a mulch that holds moisture in the gravel and creates rich soil for next spring's weeds.</p>
<p>Clear leaves and fallen branches regularly with a leaf blower or a plastic leaf rake. Avoid digging in with a heavy metal rake, which pulls up stone and disturbs the compacted layer. Keeping the surface clear means rainwater can still run off the crown rather than being held by soggy piles of leaves.</p>

<h2 id="drainage-maintenance">6. Drainage maintenance</h2>
<p>Before the first frosts, check your drainage. A gravel driveway is only as good as the system that takes water away from it. If side ditches, channels or gullies are clogged with silt, leaves or long grass, water backs up onto the drive. In winter that standing water freezes and can cause heaving and real structural damage.</p>
<ul>
<li>Clear channels, soakaway inlets and drainage grates at the edges of the drive.</li>
<li>Make sure the ends of any pipes under the driveway are free of obstructions.</li>
<li>If heavy autumn rain is washing out parts of the drive, ask your installer about cut-off drains across the surface or larger stone along the edges to slow the runoff.</li>
</ul>
<p>If a ditch, culvert or drainage pipe sits where your driveway meets a public road, check with your local highway authority before altering it, as works there may need their consent.</p>

<h2 id="winter-snow">7. Winter snow clearing</h2>
<p>Winter brings the biggest risk of losing stone. When you plough or clear snow, it is very easy to scoop up driveway gravel and throw it onto the lawn.</p>
<p>To avoid this, raise the blade or adjust the skids on a snow plough or snow blower so the blade sits slightly above the stones; check the equipment's manual for how to set it. Leaving a thin layer of packed snow is no bad thing, as it protects the gravel underneath. If you are shovelling by hand, skim the snow rather than scraping the ground.</p>
<p>Use de-icing salt sparingly: the brine can soak into the base and make freeze-thaw damage worse, and runoff can harm nearby lawns and planting. For grip, a little grit or sand on the main walking route is usually enough.</p>

<h2 id="grading">8. Regrading the surface</h2>
<p>Grading levels the surface and restores the crown. How often it is needed depends on traffic and weather, but many drives benefit from it at least once a year. Late spring or early autumn is usually best, when the ground is damp but not waterlogged.</p>
<p>On a long drive, a contractor may use a box blade or grader behind a tractor. For a shorter one, a homemade drag works: a heavy section of chain-link fence weighted down with concrete blocks, pulled slowly behind a garden tractor or vehicle. If you try this, keep the blocks well secured, keep speed very low, make sure no one stands near or rides on the drag, and keep children and pets well away. Either way, the aim is to pull gravel back from the edges towards the centre, rebuilding the crown and smoothing out washboarding, the ripples that form where vehicles brake or accelerate.</p>

<h2 id="top-dressing">9. Top dressing</h2>
<p>Over time stones are crushed to dust, carried off on tyres or pressed into the layers below. Every few years, depending on traffic and weather, most gravel drives need a top dressing: a fresh, thin layer of stone that restores the look and the surface level.</p>
<p>Match the new stone to the existing material in size, colour and type. A well-built gravel drive is usually made in layers: larger stone at the bottom for strength and drainage, then crushed stone with fines that compacts into a firm base, then the decorative top stone. Your installer will specify the stone and depths for your ground. Spring is a good time to top up, so the new stone can settle and compact over the summer.</p>

<blockquote><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Keep a note of exactly which stone was used on your drive, and where it came from, when it is first laid. When you top up, matching the colour and size is far easier, and the drive will not end up patchy.</p></blockquote>

<h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>
<h3>How often should I add new gravel to my driveway?</h3>
<p>Every few years, depending on traffic, the local weather and how well the drive was built in the first place. If you can see the base layer or the soil underneath, it is time for a top dressing.</p>
<h3>Can I use an ordinary garden rake?</h3>
<p>A garden rake is fine for small patches. For general upkeep, a wide landscape rake or a drag spreads stone and fines more evenly, which keeps the surface stable.</p>
<h3>What is the best way to stop potholes coming back?</h3>
<p>Use the right material and compact it well. Never fill a pothole with clean, rounded stones, because they will not lock together. Use crushed stone with fines, compact it in thin layers and make sure the crown sheds water so it does not sit in the hole.</p>
<h3>Is it better to plough or blow snow off a gravel drive?</h3>
<p>A snow blower is usually gentler on gravel, provided the skids are set so the auger stays clear of the stones. Ploughing is quicker but needs care so the blade does not scrape the drive bare and push stone onto the lawn.</p>
<h3>Why is my gravel driveway turning to mud?</h3>
<p>This usually means the sub-base has failed or there are not enough fines in the surface. If there is no separation layer, such as a geotextile membrane, between soil and stone, the two gradually mix. The fix is usually to dig out the soft area and rebuild it with a proper base, which is a job for your installer if the area is large.</p>

<h2 id="final-thoughts">Final thoughts</h2>
<p>Gravel rewards little and often: a spring walk round, regular clearing, careful snow clearing and a top-up every few years will keep it in good shape for a long time. Gravel is also permeable, which is useful in England, where a new or replacement hard surface over 5 m² in a front garden generally needs planning permission unless it is permeable or drains to a permeable area; check with your local authority, and note that a new dropped kerb needs council permission. If you are planning a new drive, read about the <a href="/blog/gravel-driveway-mistakes">gravel driveway mistakes</a> that cost homeowners money and <a href="/blog/gravel-driveway-cost">what drives gravel driveway cost</a>, or compare the options in our guide to <a href="/blog/paver-driveway-pros-and-cons">paver driveway pros and cons</a>. You can also explore our <a href="/services/balcony-terrace-decoration">balcony and terrace decoration</a> service, or <a href="/book-consultation">book a consultation</a> to plan your outdoor space with a designer.</p>
